 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Schools</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest schools and attendance zones</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The attendance area belongs to Horry County Schools: Carolina Forest High School, Ten Oaks and Ocean Bay middle schools, and five elementary schools. Carolina Forest High enrolls about 3,181 students, the fifth-largest public high school in South Carolina, ranked 33rd in the state by U.S. News, with 80 percent math and 91 percent reading proficiency against state averages of 43 and 52.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The growth showed up in the classrooms first: by 2024, Carolina Forest Elementary ran at 136 percent of capacity, Ocean Bay at 144, and River Oaks at 166. The district's answer opened in August 2025: Ten Oaks Elementary ($62.3 million) and Pine Island Elementary ($65.7 million), 1,200 seats each, with 1,920 students reassigned to fill them. Which school serves which subdivision is set by the district's attendance maps, and lines just moved, so we check the <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/newsroom/2024-2025-news-pages/cfnewschools" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">current district maps</a> on every showing rather than trusting what a home is advertised as.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">Districtwide, Horry County Schools posted 38 of 57 schools rated Excellent or Good on the 2025 state report cards, with none Unsatisfactory, and an 89.6 percent graduation rate.</p></div></section>
 
